# ============================================================== SKILLS
ch = p.chapter("skills", "Historical skills")
L = lesson(ch, "skills", "Sources, bias and essay writing",
    ["Distinguish primary and secondary sources, written and oral", "Evaluate a source by origin, purpose and content", "Plan and write a structured historical essay"],
    [("definition", "Sources", "A **primary source** comes from the time studied (a letter, treaty, photograph, oral testimony). A **secondary source** is written later by someone who studies the past (a textbook, a historian’s article). **Oral tradition** is an important source for African history: it is passed down by griots, elders and court historians and must be checked against other evidence like any source."),
     ("methode", "Evaluating a source: O-P-C", "Ask three questions. **Origin:** who made it, when, where? **Purpose:** why was it made, for whom? **Content:** what does it say and what does it leave out? Then judge its **reliability** (can we trust it?) and **usefulness** (does it help with this question?). A biased source can still be useful, for example to show an opinion."),
     ("methode", "Essay structure", "**Introduction:** answer the question directly and outline your line of argument. **Body paragraphs:** point, evidence (dates, names, events), explanation linking it to the question. **Counter-argument:** another view. **Conclusion:** a reasoned judgement. Define the command word: *describe* (what), *explain* (why), *assess* or *to what extent* (weigh both sides and decide)."),
     ("pieges", "Common mistakes", "- Writing everything you know instead of answering the question.\n- Assuming a biased source is worthless, or a primary source is always true.\n- Describing events without explaining causes and consequences.\n- Giving dates and names without being sure of them: be accurate or be general.")],
    [("Example 1 — Evaluate a source", "Imaginary extract written for practice: ‘Our company has brought order and trade to the coast; the chiefs are grateful.’ — report by a trading company agent to his head office, 1880s. Evaluate its reliability.",
      ["Origin: an agent of the company, writing to his employers.", "Purpose: to show success and protect his job and the company’s interests.", "Content: claims gratitude but gives no African testimony.", "Judgement: useful for company attitudes, not reliable evidence of what chiefs felt."],
      "Biased by purpose; useful for company views, weak on African opinion."),
     ("Example 2 — Plan an essay", "Plan an answer to: ‘To what extent was trade the main cause of European colonisation of Africa?’",
      ["Introduction: trade and raw materials were important but not the only cause.", "Para 1: economic causes (markets, raw materials, investment).", "Para 2: political and strategic causes (rivalry, prestige).", "Para 3: other causes (missionary, humanitarian arguments, technology).", "Conclusion: economic motives were strong but linked to political rivalry; weigh them."],
      "A balanced plan with a clear judgement.")],
    [M("A letter written by a soldier during a war is", "A primary source", ["A secondary source", "A modern textbook", "A map made today"], "It comes from the period studied."),
     TF("Oral tradition has no value for historians.", False, "It is an important source in African history, evaluated like any other."),
     MT("Match each word to its meaning.", [("Origin", "Who made the source and when"), ("Purpose", "Why the source was made"), ("Bias", "A one-sided view"), ("Reliability", "How far a source can be trusted")], "Key source-evaluation terms."),
     M("‘To what extent’ requires you to", "Weigh arguments and reach a judgement", ["Only list facts", "Give a definition", "Copy the source"], "It calls for evaluation."),
     O("Explain why a government report and a village elder’s account of the same event may differ.", "The government report was written to explain or defend official policy and may stress order and success, while the elder remembers the experience of the community and may stress loss or resistance. Each has a different purpose, viewpoint and level of access to information, so comparing them gives a fuller picture.",
       "Different origin/purpose (2); different viewpoint (1); value of comparing (1)."),
     O("A source states that ‘all the people welcomed the new rulers’. Write a paragraph evaluating it for a historian, using O-P-C.", "A claim about ‘all’ the people should make us cautious. We need to know who wrote it: if it was a ruler or an official, the purpose was probably to justify rule. Its content gives no evidence of the views of ordinary people, and different groups usually react differently. It is useful for the official attitude but needs to be checked against other sources such as oral accounts and letters.",
       "Origin (1); purpose (1); content and limits (1); cross-checking and judgement (1).")],
    [("A secondary source is", "Written later by someone studying the past", ["Always inaccurate", "Always a letter", "A treaty signed at the time"], "Historians’ accounts are secondary."),
     ("Cross-referencing means", "Comparing sources to check them", ["Copying the best source", "Ignoring differences", "Using only one source"], "It tests reliability."),
     ("A good introduction", "Answers the question and states the line of argument", ["Tells a long story", "Repeats the question only", "Contains no argument"], "It gives direction."),
     ("Bias in a source", "Can still make it useful", ["Makes it useless", "Proves it false", "Means it is secondary"], "Bias reveals attitudes."),
     ("‘Assess’ asks you to", "Weigh up and judge", ["List only", "Define only", "Describe only"], "A command word for evaluation.")],
    (flow(["Origin: who, when, where?", "Purpose: why made, for whom?", "Content: what it says and omits", "Judgement: reliability and usefulness"], 440, "blue", 13, 320),
     "The O-P-C method for evaluating a source.", "Four boxes with arrows: origin, purpose, content and judgement."),
    ["Command-word definitions follow general practice: check the GCE Board’s own list.", "Sources in this lesson are imaginary, written for practice; add real extracts only from permitted sources."])

# ============================================================== AFRICA BEFORE 1884
ch = p.chapter("precolonial", "Africa before colonial rule")
tl1 = timeline(700, 1700, events=[(1324, "Mansa Musa’s pilgrimage"), (1591, "Songhai defeated")],
               periods=[(750, 1100, "Ghana", "orange"), (1230, 1500, "Mali", "blue"), (1460, 1591, "Songhai", "green")], w=480, h=240)
L = lesson(ch, "states", "Pre-colonial states and societies",
    ["Describe the main West African empires and their trade", "Describe states and societies in Cameroon before colonial rule", "Identify the features of centralised and decentralised societies"],
    [("definition", "The Sahelian empires", "Gold, salt, cloth, ivory and slaves crossed the Sahara by camel caravan. Control of this **trans-Saharan trade** made three successive empires powerful: **Ghana** (from about the 8th to the 11th century), **Mali** (about the 13th to 15th century) and **Songhai** (15th–16th century), with centres such as Gao and Timbuktu famous for trade and Islamic learning. Mansa Musa of Mali made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca in about 1324. Songhai was defeated by a Moroccan army in 1591."),
     ("retenir", "Lake Chad region and the forest", "**Kanem-Bornu**, near Lake Chad, was a long-lived state controlling trans-Saharan routes. In the forest and coastal zones, states such as Oyo and Benin and, later, Asante had kings, councils, armies and trading networks. Societies without kings, organised by age sets, lineages and councils, also existed."),
     ("retenir", "Cameroon", "The **Bamum** kingdom with its capital at Foumban and the chiefdoms (fondoms) of the western grassfields, such as many Bamileke and Tikar groups, were organised states under rulers with courts and titled officials. In the north, Fulbe (Fulani) emirates were founded in the 19th century after the jihad movement led from Sokoto (1804). On the coast, the **Duala** acted as middlemen in trade with Europeans. Each had its own politics, economy and culture."),
     ("pieges", "Avoid these errors", "- Africa had complex states and societies long before European rule.\n- Do not treat empires as having fixed modern borders: control was strongest near centres and trade routes.\n- Dates for early empires are approximate; use ‘about’ or ‘c.’ unless you are certain.")],
    [("Example 1 — Why trade made Mali wealthy", "Explain why control of the trans-Saharan trade made the Mali empire rich.",
      ["Gold came from the south and salt from the Sahara; both were in high demand.", "Mali’s rulers taxed caravans and traders passing through their lands.", "Wealth paid for armies, cities and pilgrimage, strengthening the state."],
      "Taxing the gold-salt trade gave revenue and power."),
     ("Example 2 — Compare two types of society", "Compare a centralised kingdom with a society ruled by councils and age sets.",
      ["Kingdom: a ruler, court and titled officials, taxes and an army.", "Council-based society: decisions by elders and councils, authority shared through lineage and age-grade.", "Both had laws and ways to settle disputes."],
      "Centralised authority versus shared authority; both organised.")],
    [M("Which was traded across the Sahara in exchange for gold?", "Salt", ["Oil", "Cars", "Machines"], "Salt came from the Sahara and was valuable in the savanna."),
     TF("Songhai was defeated by a Moroccan army in 1591.", True, "This is the standard date for the fall of the empire."),
     MT("Match the state to its region.", [("Mali", "Western Sahel"), ("Kanem-Bornu", "Near Lake Chad"), ("Bamum kingdom", "Cameroon grassfields"), ("Duala", "Cameroon coast")], "General locations."),
     N("Mansa Musa’s pilgrimage was in about 1324. How many years before 1591 was that?", 267, "1591 − 1324 = 267.", 0, "years"),
     O("Explain why the Duala were important in trade between Europeans and the interior.", "The Duala lived at the coast, where European ships arrived. They acted as middlemen, buying goods from the interior and selling them to European traders while receiving manufactured goods in return. This gave them wealth and influence and a strong interest in keeping control of the trade.",
       "Coastal location (1); middlemen role (1); wealth/influence (1); interest in monopoly (1)."),
     O("Assess the importance of trade in the growth of West African empires.", "Trade was central: control of the trans-Saharan routes brought taxes, wealth and weapons, helped by gold and salt. Cities such as Timbuktu grew rich and spread Islam and learning. But other factors also mattered: strong leaders, armies, agriculture and administration. Trade enabled the empires but did not alone create them.",
       "Role of trade (2); other factors (2); judgement (1).")],
    [("Timbuktu was famous for", "Trade and Islamic learning", ["Oil exports", "Rubber plantations", "Railway workshops"], "A centre of the trans-Saharan trade."),
     ("Which empire came first?", "Ghana", ["Songhai", "Mali", "Asante"], "Ghana is the earliest of the three Sahelian empires."),
     ("Fulbe emirates in the north were founded after", "The 19th-century jihad movement", ["The Berlin Conference", "World War I", "Independence"], "The Sokoto jihad began in 1804."),
     ("Bamum kingdom’s capital was", "Foumban", ["Douala", "Garoua", "Limbe"], "Foumban in the West region."),
     ("Oral tradition is", "A way of keeping history through speech", ["Always written", "Always false", "Only modern"], "Passed down by speakers.")],
    (tl1, "Approximate periods of three West African empires.", "A timeline from 700 to 1700 showing Ghana, Mali and Songhai periods, and the dates 1324 and 1591."),
    ["Verify: Ghana c. 8th–11th century; Mali c. 13th–15th; Songhai 15th–16th; Mansa Musa’s pilgrimage c. 1324; defeat of Songhai 1591; Sokoto jihad 1804.", "Check how the syllabus presents Cameroon’s pre-colonial peoples (Duala, Bamum, Fulbe, Tikar, Bamileke)."])

tl2 = timeline(1500, 1900, events=[(1807, "Britain bans the trade"), (1833, "Slavery abolished"), (1848, "France abolishes")], periods=[(1500, 1860, "Trans-Atlantic slave trade", "red")], w=480, h=240)
L = lesson(ch, "slave-trade", "The trans-Atlantic slave trade and its abolition",
    ["Describe the origins, organisation and effects of the trans-Atlantic slave trade", "Explain the reasons for abolition", "Describe the settlement of freed slaves and the shift to ‘legitimate trade’"],
    [("definition", "The trade", "From the 15th century European traders carried captive Africans to work on plantations in the Americas. More than ten million people were taken across the Atlantic in over three centuries, most in the 18th century. The **triangular trade** linked Europe (manufactured goods), Africa (captives) and the Americas (sugar, cotton, tobacco). African rulers and merchants took part in the trade, which was enforced by European demand and by the guns and goods offered."),
     ("retenir", "Effects", "**On Africa:** loss of many young people, insecurity and wars to capture people, damaged societies and economies in affected regions; some states grew powerful by controlling the trade. **In the Americas:** forced labour and the growth of plantation economies and large African-descended populations. **In Europe:** profits for traders and ports, which helped finance later development."),
     ("retenir", "Abolition", "Causes: the campaigns of abolitionists (including freed Africans such as Olaudah Equiano), religious and humanitarian movements, resistance and revolt by enslaved people, and changing economic interests in Britain. **Britain banned the slave trade in 1807** and abolished slavery in most of its empire in **1833** (effective 1834). The Royal Navy patrolled West African waters. France abolished slavery finally in 1848; the United States in 1865."),
     ("retenir", "After abolition", "Settlements for freed people were founded, including Freetown in Sierra Leone and Liberia. Europeans promoted **‘legitimate trade’** in palm oil, groundnuts and rubber instead of people. On the Cameroon coast, Duala and other coastal communities traded palm oil and the missions, such as the Baptist mission under Alfred Saker, established Victoria (now Limbe) in 1858 as a settlement for freed people."),
     ("attention", "Handle with care", "- The trade was driven by European demand but African participation varied by region and period; avoid blaming one group.\n- Abolition of the trade (1807) is not the same as the end of slavery (1833 and later).\n- Slavery and the slave trade continued in some places after abolition laws.")],
    [("Example 1 — Triangular trade", "Describe the three sides of the triangular trade.",
      ["Europe to Africa: manufactured goods, guns, cloth.", "Africa to the Americas: captive Africans (the Middle Passage).", "Americas to Europe: sugar, cotton, tobacco and other plantation goods."], "A cycle linking three continents."),
     ("Example 2 — Why abolish?", "Give two reasons why Britain abolished the slave trade in 1807.",
      ["Humanitarian campaigns by abolitionists and churches changed opinion.", "Resistance and revolts by enslaved people raised the cost and risk.", "Economic interests were shifting, and some argued other trade would pay better."], "Moral campaigns and changing conditions.")],
    [M("In what year did Britain ban the trade in slaves?", "1807", ["1833", "1884", "1960"], "The Slave Trade Act of 1807."),
     N("Britain abolished the trade in 1807 and slavery in most of its empire in 1833. How many years later?", 26, "1833 − 1807 = 26.", 0, "years"),
     TF("‘Legitimate trade’ meant trade in goods such as palm oil instead of in people.", True, "That is the usual meaning."),
     M("Freetown was founded as a settlement for", "Freed slaves", ["German soldiers", "Oil workers", "Miners"], "Freed people were settled in Sierra Leone."),
     O("State three effects of the slave trade on African societies.", "Loss of young and productive people reduced population and labour; insecurity and warfare grew as people were captured; trade in people and guns changed the balance of power and damaged other economic activities in affected areas; families were broken. Some coastal states gained wealth from the trade.",
       "One mark per valid effect, max 3."),
     O("Assess the importance of humanitarian campaigns in bringing about the abolition of the slave trade.", "Abolitionists and churches changed public opinion with pamphlets, petitions and testimony from freed Africans, which put pressure on the government. But resistance by the enslaved, changing economic interests and the interests of naval power also mattered. Humanitarian campaigns were a major factor, though not the only one.",
       "Role of campaigns (2); other factors (2); judgement (1).")],
    [("The Middle Passage was", "The voyage across the Atlantic", ["A mountain pass", "A Sahara route", "A river"], "The captives’ sea journey."),
     ("Alfred Saker was associated with", "The Baptist mission on the Cameroon coast", ["The Basel Mission only", "The German army", "The League of Nations"], "He worked in Douala and founded Victoria."),
     ("Slavery in most of the British Empire was abolished in", "1833", ["1807", "1884", "1914"], "The Slavery Abolition Act."),
     ("Palm oil was important in", "Legitimate trade", ["The triangular trade only", "The Cold War", "The League"], "It replaced trade in people."),
     ("Olaudah Equiano was", "A freed African who campaigned for abolition", ["A German governor", "A Duala king", "A UN official"], "He wrote an account of his life.")],
    (tl2, "Slave trade and abolition dates.", "Timeline from 1500 to 1900 with the trans-Atlantic slave trade period and abolition dates 1807, 1833 and 1848."),
    ["Verify: 1807 Slave Trade Act; 1833 Slavery Abolition Act (effective 1834); France 1848; USA 1865; Victoria founded 1858; Saker’s activities; ‘more than ten million’ is a rounded scholarly estimate.", "Teach this topic sensitively; follow the official syllabus emphasis."])

L = lesson(ch, "explorers-missions", "Explorers, missionaries and the road to colonial rule",
    ["Describe the roles of explorers, missionaries and traders", "Explain how the coast of Cameroon attracted European interest", "Assess the link between these groups and colonial conquest"],
    [("definition", "Explorers", "In the 19th century European explorers travelled in Africa to map rivers and routes: **Mungo Park** on the Niger (1790s), the **Lander brothers** (1830, the mouth of the Niger), **Heinrich Barth** in the Sahel and Lake Chad region, and **David Livingstone** in central and southern Africa. Their reports gave information about peoples, resources and routes, and increased European interest."),
     ("retenir", "Missionaries", "Missionaries built churches, schools and clinics. Baptist missionaries such as Joseph Merrick and Alfred Saker worked on the Cameroon coast from the 1840s; the Basel Mission, Catholic missions and others followed. They translated the Bible, taught reading, and introduced new ideas, but they also sometimes prepared the way for colonial rule. Africans took many of the teaching roles."),
     ("retenir", "Traders and treaties", "European traders from Britain and Germany bought palm oil, ivory and rubber at the Cameroon coast. Duala rulers, such as King Bell and King Akwa, controlled the trade and in the 1870s sent letters to Britain asking for protection and a settlement of their affairs. In July 1884, German representative Gustav Nachtigal signed treaties with the Duala chiefs, beginning German rule. Many details are debated by historians."),
     ("attention", "Be balanced", "Explorers, missionaries and traders were not all the same: some respected African societies, others did not. Africans were not passive; they used trade and diplomacy to defend their interests. Avoid saying that missionaries were only agents of colonialism or only helpers of Africans.")],
    [("Example 1 — Importance of explorers", "Explain why explorers’ reports increased European interest in Africa.",
      ["They described rivers and routes into the interior, showing how to reach inland markets.", "They described resources such as gold, ivory and palm oil.", "Their books were popular and increased public interest at home."], "Information and publicity encouraged commercial and political interest."),
     ("Example 2 — Role of missionaries", "Give two positive and one critical point about the work of missionaries in Cameroon’s coast.",
      ["Positive: schools taught reading and writing; the Bible was translated into local languages.", "Positive: clinics and new skills were introduced.", "Critical: the missions sometimes undermined local religion and customs and supported colonial authority."], "A mixed record that needs balanced treatment.")],
    [M("Which explorer reached the mouth of the Niger in 1830, with his brother?", "Richard Lander", ["Gustav Nachtigal", "Leopold II", "Bismarck"], "The Lander brothers traced the Niger to the sea."),
     TF("Alfred Saker worked in Cameroon in the 19th century.", True, "He was a Baptist missionary on the coast."),
     MT("Match the group to its main interest.", [("Explorers", "Mapping and information"), ("Missionaries", "Spreading Christianity and education"), ("Traders", "Profit from palm oil and ivory"), ("Duala rulers", "Control of the coastal trade")], "General interests, not exclusive."),
     M("Which German representative signed treaties with Duala chiefs in 1884?", "Gustav Nachtigal", ["David Livingstone", "Mungo Park", "Joseph Merrick"], "He was sent to declare a protectorate."),
     O("Explain how missionaries influenced education in Cameroon.", "Missionaries opened schools to teach reading, writing and arithmetic, translated religious texts into local languages, and trained African teachers and clerks. The mission schools provided many of the earliest educated Africans and influenced the later development of education.", "Schools (1); translation (1); training (1); lasting effect (1)."),
     O("To what extent did explorers and missionaries prepare the way for European colonisation of Africa?", "They gave information about routes, peoples and resources, built ties with African rulers and sometimes urged government protection of their interests. This helped colonisation. But the decisive causes were economic competition, rivalry between states and the new technologies. Africans also shaped events. So they contributed, but they were not the main cause.", "Contribution (2); other causes (2); judgement (1).")],
    [("Mungo Park explored", "The Niger River", ["The Nile", "Lake Chad", "The Zambezi"], "He travelled to the Niger in the 1790s."),
     ("The Basel Mission in Cameroon came", "After the Baptists", ["Before the Baptists", "In 1950", "In 1700"], "It arrived in the 1880s."),
     ("The Duala trade was mainly in", "Palm oil and ivory", ["Oil and gas", "Cars", "Machines"], "Typical goods."),
     ("David Livingstone is known for", "Exploring central and southern Africa", ["Founding Douala", "Signing the Berlin Act", "Leading the UN"], "He worked in central and southern Africa."),
     ("A protectorate is", "A territory controlled by a stronger power", ["A free state", "A trade fair", "A kind of mission"], "Control without full annexation.")],
    (flow(["Explorers map routes and resources", "Missionaries and traders establish contacts", "Rivalry among European powers grows", "Treaties and claims lead to colonial rule"], 440, "blue", 13, 340),
     "How contact led to colonial rule (simplified).", "Four boxes in a chain: explorers, missionaries and traders, rivalry, and treaties leading to colonial rule."),
    ["Verify: Park 1790s; Landers 1830; Nachtigal treaty with Duala chiefs July 1884 (12 July is often given); Duala letters to Britain in the 1870s; Saker, Merrick and the Baptist mission; Basel Mission arrival 1886.", "Check the names used for Duala rulers (Bell, Akwa) as in the syllabus."])

# ============================================================== SCRAMBLE AND KAMERUN
ch = p.chapter("scramble", "The Scramble for Africa and German Kamerun")
tl3 = timeline(1870, 1914, events=[(1884, "Berlin Conference opens"), (1885, "Berlin Act"), (1898, "Fashoda"), (1914, "Ethiopia, Liberia free")], periods=[(1880, 1914, "Scramble", "orange")], w=480, h=240)
L = lesson(ch, "berlin", "The Scramble for Africa and the Berlin Conference",
    ["Explain the economic, political and social causes of the Scramble", "Describe the Berlin Conference (1884–85) and its rules", "Assess the consequences for Africa"],
    [("definition", "Causes", "**Economic:** raw materials (rubber, palm oil, minerals), new markets and investment. **Political and strategic:** national prestige, rivalry between Britain, France and Germany, control of trade routes. **Social and humanitarian:** missionary movements and the claim to end the slave trade and ‘civilise’ Africa. **Technology:** steamships, quinine against malaria, rifles and the telegraph made inland conquest possible."),
     ("retenir", "The Berlin Conference", "Called by Germany’s chancellor Otto von Bismarck, it met in Berlin from **November 1884 to February 1885** with European powers, the United States and the Ottoman Empire present; no African ruler took part. Agreements: free trade and navigation on the Congo and Niger, the recognition of King Leopold II’s Congo claim, and a rule that claims to coastal territory needed **effective occupation** and notification to other powers."),
     ("retenir", "Consequences", "The conference did not itself divide Africa, but it set rules for competition. By 1914 almost all of Africa was under European control; only **Ethiopia and Liberia** remained independent. Borders drawn in Europe cut across peoples or joined unlike ones. Africans resisted in many places and used diplomacy, but were often defeated by superior weapons."),
     ("pieges", "Common mistakes", "- The Berlin Conference did not ‘start’ colonialism or draw the whole map: it set rules; borders were agreed later.\n- African rulers were not invited to the conference.\n- Effective occupation concerned coastal claims and meant real presence, not just flags.")],
    [("Example 1 — Rank causes", "Which cause was most important? Argue briefly for economic causes.",
      ["Industrial Europe needed raw materials and markets.", "Governments protected trade and investment.", "Political rivalry was partly rivalry over economic gain, so economics linked to many other causes."], "Economic causes are strong but connected to others."),
     ("Example 2 — Effects of arbitrary borders", "Explain one long-term effect of the way Africa’s borders were drawn.",
      ["Borders ignored language, culture and trade routes.", "Peoples were split among several countries, while unlike groups were joined.", "This created challenges for national unity and cross-border cooperation after independence."], "Divided peoples and national-unity challenges.")],
    [M("Which leader hosted the Berlin Conference?", "Otto von Bismarck", ["Queen Victoria", "Napoleon", "Leopold II"], "He was Germany’s chancellor."),
     TF("African rulers took part in the Berlin Conference.", False, "None was invited."),
     MT("Match the cause with its type.", [("Raw materials", "Economic"), ("National prestige", "Political"), ("Spreading Christianity", "Social/religious"), ("Quinine", "Technological")], "Standard classification."),
     N("The Berlin Conference opened in 1884. By 1914, how many years had passed?", 30, "1914 − 1884 = 30.", 0, "years"),
     O("Explain the principle of ‘effective occupation’.", "A European power could claim African coastal land only if it showed real control there, with administration, officials or troops, and told the other powers. It was meant to reduce disputes, but it encouraged a rush to occupy land and sign treaties.", "Meaning (1); requirement to notify (1); aim (1); effect (1)."),
     O("Assess the view that economic motives were the main cause of the Scramble for Africa.", "Economic motives were important: Europe needed raw materials, markets and secure investment. But political rivalry and national prestige, strategic routes, humanitarian and missionary arguments and new technology were also causes. In many cases political competition decided where and when claims were made. Economic motives were a main cause but not the only one.", "Economic causes (2); other causes (2); judgement (1).")],
    [("The Berlin Conference ended in", "1885", ["1914", "1960", "1900"], "It ran from November 1884 to February 1885."),
     ("Two African states independent in 1914 were", "Ethiopia and Liberia", ["Nigeria and Ghana", "Cameroon and Chad", "Egypt and Kenya"], "They were the exceptions."),
     ("King Leopold II’s claim was to", "The Congo", ["Cameroon", "Egypt", "Kenya"], "The Congo Free State."),
     ("A technological cause of conquest was", "The Maxim gun and quinine", ["The telephone only", "Radio", "Television"], "They gave advantages in conquest."),
     ("Effective occupation required", "Real control and notification", ["Only a flag", "A treaty with Africans", "A mission"], "To validate coastal claims.")],
    (tl3, "The Scramble for Africa, 1870–1914.", "Timeline with events 1884 Berlin Conference opens, 1885 Berlin Act, 1898 Fashoda and 1914 only Ethiopia and Liberia independent."),
    ["Verify: conference dates (15 Nov 1884–26 Feb 1885); Bismarck host; participants; effective occupation; Fashoda 1898; Ethiopia and Liberia in 1914.", "Check emphasis of the syllabus on causes."])

tl4 = timeline(1880, 1925, events=[(1884, "Treaty with Duala"), (1911, "Neukamerun"), (1914, "WWI begins"), (1916, "Germans leave")], periods=[(1884, 1916, "German Kamerun", "blue")], w=480, h=240)
L = lesson(ch, "kamerun", "German Kamerun, 1884–1916",
    ["Describe the establishment of German rule", "Describe German administration and economy", "Explain African responses and resistance"],
    [("definition", "Establishment", "In July 1884 Germany made treaties with Duala chiefs and declared a protectorate, **Kamerun** (1884). Over the next years German officials, soldiers and companies pushed inland, signing treaties and fighting those who resisted. In **1911**, after an agreement with France, Kamerun was enlarged by territory to the south and east (**‘Neukamerun’**)."),
     ("retenir", "Administration and economy", "A governor represented the German emperor, with district officers and a colonial army. Germans used taxes and labour requirements. **Plantations** of cocoa, bananas, rubber and palm oil developed on fertile land, especially around Mount Cameroon. Railways and roads from Douala to the interior were begun to carry exports. Missions ran many schools, and German was taught in some."),
     ("retenir", "African responses", "Africans responded in different ways: some rulers cooperated, others resisted. Duala leaders protested against plans to move them from their land; their leader **Rudolf Duala Manga Bell** was executed in **August 1914** on a charge of treason. Resistance to German expansion also occurred inland and in the grassfields. Plantation labour and taxation were widely resented."),
     ("attention", "Be careful", "- Neukamerun came in 1911, not in 1884.\n- German rule lasted about 30 years, ending during the First World War.\n- Do not describe African societies as passive: they used negotiation, resistance and adaptation.")],
    [("Example 1 — Length of rule", "German rule began in 1884 and the Germans left in 1916. How long did it last?",
      ["Subtract: 1916 − 1884.", "= 32 years."], "About 32 years."),
     ("Example 2 — Why plantations?", "Explain why plantations developed around Mount Cameroon.",
      ["Volcanic soils are fertile and rainfall is high.", "The coast made export to Europe easy.", "Colonial authorities gave companies land and labour."], "Soil, climate and access to export, plus colonial policy.")],
    [N("German rule in Kamerun lasted from 1884 to 1916. How many years?", 32, "1916 − 1884 = 32.", 0, "years"),
     M("What does ‘Neukamerun’ refer to?", "Territory added to Kamerun in 1911", ["A German missionary society", "A river", "A railway"], "An enlargement following an agreement with France."),
     TF("Rudolf Duala Manga Bell was executed in 1914.", True, "August 1914 is the usual date."),
     M("Which crop was typically grown on German plantations near Mount Cameroon?", "Banana", ["Wheat", "Tea in the Sahel", "Maize only"], "Bananas, cocoa, rubber and palm products."),
     O("Describe two ways in which Germany tried to make its colony profitable.", "Germany encouraged large plantations of cocoa, bananas and rubber, with African labour, and built roads and railways to carry exports to Douala. It also taxed Africans and sometimes required labour.", "Two methods (2); explanation (2)."),
     O("Explain why some African communities resisted German rule and how the Germans responded.", "Communities resisted because of loss of land and independence, taxes and forced labour. Some, like Duala leaders, protested against moving their settlements. The Germans used force, punitive expeditions and in some cases executions to put down opposition, while also finding cooperation from some rulers.", "Reasons (2); German response (2); balanced note (1).")],
    [("The German colony was called", "Kamerun", ["Cameroon only", "Togoland", "Nigeria"], "German spelling."),
     ("The main port of German Kamerun was", "Douala", ["Garoua", "Bamenda", "Maroua"], "Its main port and trading centre."),
     ("German schools in Kamerun were often run by", "Missions", ["Police", "Banks", "Railway firms"], "Mission schools were important."),
     ("Duala land protests were linked to", "Plans to relocate Duala", ["The Berlin Act", "The Cold War", "Trusteeship"], "Land disputes."),
     ("German rule ended", "During the First World War", ["In 1960", "In 1884", "In 1945"], "The Germans withdrew in 1916.")],
    (tl4, "German Kamerun, 1884–1916.", "Timeline from 1880 to 1925 with 1884 treaty, 1911 Neukamerun, 1914 WWI begins and 1916 Germans leave."),
    ["Verify: treaty July 1884; Neukamerun 1911; Rudolf Duala Manga Bell executed August 1914; main crops; railway names and dates (not given here).", "Handle African resistance accounts using sources approved by the syllabus."])

# ============================================================== WWI
ch = p.chapter("wwi", "The First World War and its effect on Cameroon")
tl5 = timeline(1905, 1925, events=[(1914, "Sarajevo, war starts"), (1916, "Kamerun partitioned"), (1918, "Armistice"), (1919, "Versailles")], periods=[(1914, 1918, "First World War", "red")], w=480, h=240)
L = lesson(ch, "ww1-causes", "Causes of the First World War (outline)",
    ["Describe the long-term causes: alliances, militarism, imperial rivalry, nationalism", "Explain the immediate cause and the outbreak of war", "Outline the war in Africa"],
    [("definition", "Long-term causes", "**Alliances:** Europe divided into the Triple Alliance (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy) and the Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain). **Militarism:** an arms race, especially in armies and navies. **Imperial rivalry:** competition for colonies and trade. **Nationalism:** pride, and the ambitions of national groups in the Balkans."),
     ("retenir", "Outbreak", "On **28 June 1914** Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, was assassinated in Sarajevo. Austria-Hungary blamed Serbia and declared war in late July. Alliances drew in Russia, Germany and France. When Germany invaded Belgium, Britain declared war on **4 August 1914**. The war ended with the armistice of **11 November 1918**."),
     ("retenir", "Africa in the war", "Fighting took place in Africa because the powers’ colonies were also at war: in Togoland, Kamerun, German South West Africa and German East Africa. Africans served as soldiers and carriers and suffered requisitioning and hardship. The war ended German colonial rule in Africa, and the colonies were redistributed under League of Nations mandates."),
     ("pieges", "Avoid these errors", "- The assassination was the trigger, not the only cause.\n- Do not blame one country alone: historians debate the relative responsibility.\n- Africa was not only a place of ‘sideshows’: its people and resources mattered to the war effort.")],
    [("Example 1 — Trigger versus cause", "Explain the difference between the trigger and the long-term causes of the war.",
      ["Long-term causes created tension: alliances, arms race, rivalry, nationalism.", "The trigger was the assassination at Sarajevo in June 1914.", "Without the tensions, the assassination might have remained a local crisis."], "Trigger sets off a war that long-term causes made possible."),
     ("Example 2 — How alliances widened the conflict", "Explain how alliances turned a local crisis into a general war.",
      ["Austria-Hungary declared war on Serbia.", "Russia supported Serbia, Germany supported Austria-Hungary.", "France was allied to Russia, and Britain entered after the invasion of Belgium."], "Each alliance drew in further states.")],
    [N("The war began in 1914 and the armistice was in 1918. For how many years did the war last, roughly?", 4, "1918 − 1914 = 4.", 0, "years"),
     M("Which city was the scene of the assassination in June 1914?", "Sarajevo", ["Berlin", "Paris", "Vienna"], "Archduke Franz Ferdinand was killed there."),
     TF("The First World War had no effect on Africa.", False, "Fighting took place in Africa and its colonies were redistributed."),
     MT("Match the term to its meaning.", [("Militarism", "Build-up of armed forces"), ("Alliance", "Agreement of mutual support"), ("Armistice", "Agreement to stop fighting"), ("Nationalism", "Strong feeling for one’s nation")], "Basic terms."),
     O("State three long-term causes of the First World War.", "The system of alliances, the arms race (militarism), rivalry for colonies and markets (imperialism), and nationalism, especially in the Balkans.", "One mark each, max 3."),
     O("Assess the importance of the alliance system as a cause of the First World War.", "Alliances turned a Balkan dispute into a continental war: promises of support brought in Russia, Germany and France and then Britain. They also encouraged states to feel secure and to take risks. But the arms race, imperial competition and nationalism created the tensions, and the decisions of leaders mattered. Alliances were a major cause, not the only one.", "Role of alliances (2); other causes (2); judgement (1).")],
    [("The Triple Entente included", "Britain, France and Russia", ["Germany and Italy", "Cameroon and Chad", "Egypt and Ethiopia"], "The Entente powers."),
     ("Britain entered the war after", "Germany invaded Belgium", ["Italy joined", "The Berlin Conference", "The Cold War"], "4 August 1914."),
     ("The armistice was signed on", "11 November 1918", ["28 June 1914", "1 September 1939", "1 January 1960"], "Fighting stopped."),
     ("Militarism means", "Strong military build-up", ["Peace treaties", "Free trade", "Missionary work"], "Arms race."),
     ("Fighting in Africa occurred in", "German colonies", ["Only Egypt", "Only the Sahara", "No colonies"], "Togoland, Kamerun, South West and East Africa.")],
    (tl5, "From Sarajevo to the Treaty of Versailles.", "Timeline with 1914 Sarajevo, 1916 partition of Kamerun, 1918 armistice, 1919 Versailles and the war period."),
    ["Verify: 28 June 1914; Britain’s declaration 4 August 1914; armistice 11 November 1918; Triple Alliance and Entente membership (Italy’s later switch).", "Check the weight the syllabus gives to each cause."])

tl6 = timeline(1884, 1961, events=[(1914, "Allied invasion"), (1916, "Partition"), (1922, "Mandates"), (1946, "Trusteeship")], periods=[(1884, 1916, "German", "blue"), (1916, 1961, "British and French", "orange")], w=480, h=240)
L = lesson(ch, "partition", "The First World War in Kamerun and the partition",
    ["Describe the conquest of German Kamerun by the Allies (1914–16)", "Describe the Anglo-French partition", "Explain the effects on Cameroon"],
    [("definition", "The war in Kamerun", "When war began in August 1914, British forces from Nigeria and French and Belgian forces from neighbouring colonies attacked German Kamerun. **Douala fell in September 1914**. German forces withdrew inland and into the interior; the last German strongholds, including Mora in the north, held out until **early 1916**. Many Germans and their African soldiers crossed into Spanish Guinea in early 1916."),
     ("retenir", "Partition", "In **1916** Britain and France agreed to divide Kamerun. France took the larger share, about four-fifths, in the east and south. Britain took two narrow strips along the border with Nigeria, **Northern and Southern Cameroons**, administered as part of Nigeria. The Treaty of Versailles (1919) took away Germany’s colonies, and in **1922** the League of Nations confirmed the division as mandates."),
     ("retenir", "Effects", "The partition divided peoples and trade networks and left Cameroon under two administrative systems with different languages, laws and education. Plantations were taken over, and former German plantations in the British zone were later run by the Cameroons Development Corporation (CDC, set up in 1946). The war also brought deaths, forced labour and requisitions."),
     ("pieges", "Frequent errors", "- Britain and France divided the territory; Germany did not hand it over by treaty in 1916.\n- The mandate was confirmed in 1922, six years after the 1916 partition.\n- Southern and Northern Cameroons were not one province but two separate strips administered with Nigeria.")],
    [("Example 1 — Time between events", "Find the time from the fall of Douala (1914) to the confirmation of the mandate (1922).",
      ["Subtract: 1922 − 1914.", "= 8 years."], "8 years."),
     ("Example 2 — Effect of partition", "Explain one long-term consequence of partitioning Kamerun between Britain and France.",
      ["Two colonial systems introduced different languages, laws and schools.", "These differences shaped the later political and social history of the territories.", "Families and trade across the boundary were disrupted."], "Divergent administrative systems and divided communities.")],
    [N("How many years between the Anglo-French partition (1916) and the League of Nations mandate (1922)?", 6, "1922 − 1916 = 6.", 0, "years"),
     M("Which colonial powers partitioned Kamerun?", "Britain and France", ["Germany and Belgium", "Portugal and Spain", "Italy and Russia"], "France took the larger share."),
     TF("The British zones were administered as part of Nigeria.", True, "Northern and Southern Cameroons were administered with Nigeria."),
     M("Which treaty removed Germany’s colonies?", "Treaty of Versailles", ["Treaty of Berlin 1885", "Treaty of Rome", "Treaty of Lomé"], "Versailles in 1919."),
     O("Describe the main stages of the Allied conquest of German Kamerun.", "In August 1914 Allied forces from Nigeria and French and Belgian colonies invaded. Douala fell in September 1914. German forces retreated inland, holding out in places such as Mora until early 1916, and many then withdrew into Spanish territory. The territory was then divided by Britain and France.", "Invasion (1); Douala (1); retreat/Mora (1); division (1)."),
     O("Explain the consequences of the partition of Kamerun for the peoples of Cameroon.", "Partition divided communities and trade networks along a new boundary. The French and British zones developed under different languages, laws, education and administrative systems, and the British zones were administered with Nigeria. These differences influenced later political questions about unification and identity. Economic life was also reorganised as plantations changed hands.", "Division (1); different systems (2); later effects (1); economic effects (1).")],
    [("Douala fell in", "1914", ["1884", "1960", "1922"], "September 1914."),
     ("The larger share went to", "France", ["Britain", "Belgium", "Spain"], "About four-fifths."),
     ("Mora is associated with", "The last German resistance in Kamerun", ["The Berlin Conference", "The UN Charter", "Independence"], "It held out until 1916."),
     ("The Treaty of Versailles dates from", "1919", ["1884", "1960", "1945"], "The peace settlement after WWI."),
     ("The British zones were", "Northern and Southern Cameroons", ["Douala and Yaoundé", "Chad and Niger", "Togo and Ghana"], "Two strips on the Nigerian border.")],
    (tl6, "From German to British and French administration.", "Timeline from 1884 to 1961 with German rule to 1916 then British and French rule, with 1914, 1916, 1922 and 1946."),
    ["Verify: Douala fell 27 September 1914 (commonly given); Mora surrender February 1916; Anglo-French partition 1916; ‘about four-fifths’ French share; mandates 1922; CDC created 1946.", "Check the spelling ‘Cameroons’ (British) and ‘Cameroun’ (French) as used in the syllabus."])

# ============================================================== INTER-WAR AND UN
ch = p.chapter("mandates", "Mandates, colonial rule and the United Nations")
L = lesson(ch, "mandates", "The League of Nations and the mandate system",
    ["Describe the creation of the League of Nations and the mandate system", "Explain the aims and limits of mandates", "Describe mandate rule in Cameroon"],
    [("definition", "The League and mandates", "The **League of Nations** was set up after the First World War (1920) to keep peace. Former German and Ottoman colonies were placed under the **mandate system**: a victorious power administered the territory on behalf of the League as a ‘sacred trust’ for the people’s well-being. Mandates were grouped as A, B and C according to their supposed readiness for self-rule. Cameroon and Togo were **B mandates**."),
     ("retenir", "Supervision", "Mandatory powers sent annual reports to the **Permanent Mandates Commission** of the League. In principle they had to protect inhabitants’ interests, prevent abuses such as slavery and promote welfare. In practice the League had little power, and the mandatory powers governed in much the same way as their other colonies."),
     ("retenir", "In Cameroon", "France governed **French Cameroun** from Yaoundé (as a separate territory) and Britain administered **Northern and Southern Cameroons** as part of Nigeria. Both kept up plantations and cash-crop exports, built roads and schools through missions and government, and used taxes and labour requirements."),
     ("pieges", "Be accurate", "- Mandates did not mean independence, and the League did not rule the territories directly.\n- ‘B mandate’ is a classification in the League system, not a type of colony.\n- Do not confuse the League of Nations (1920) with the United Nations (1945).")],
    [("Example 1 — The aim of the mandate", "State two aims of the mandate system.",
      ["To administer former colonies for the well-being of their inhabitants.", "To supervise the powers through reports and the Permanent Mandates Commission."], "Welfare as a ‘sacred trust’ and international supervision."),
     ("Example 2 — Limits of the system", "Explain two weaknesses of the mandate system.",
      ["The League could not enforce its decisions and relied on annual reports.", "Mandatory powers ran the territories much as their colonies, with limited African participation."], "Weak supervision and little change in practice.")],
    [M("The mandate system was run by", "The League of Nations", ["The United Nations", "The OAU", "The Commonwealth"], "It came before the UN."),
     TF("The mandate over Cameroon was confirmed in 1922.", True, "Mandates were confirmed in 1922."),
     MT("Match each body to its period.", [("League of Nations", "1920 onwards"), ("United Nations", "1945 onwards"), ("OAU", "1963 onwards"), ("African Union", "2002 onwards")], "Standard dates, to be verified."),
     M("Cameroon was classified as a", "B mandate", ["A mandate", "C mandate", "Colony of the League"], "B mandates were in central and west Africa."),
     O("Explain how the British and French mandates in Cameroon differed in administration.", "The French mandate was governed from Yaoundé as a separate territory, with direct French administration. The British areas were governed as part of Nigeria, and Britain used local rulers where possible. Languages, laws and school systems also differed.", "French system (1); British system (1); language/law (1); difference summarised (1)."),
     O("Assess the effectiveness of the League of Nations in protecting the peoples of mandated territories.", "The League set standards, required reports and gave a forum for petitions. But it had no army and no power to compel the mandatory powers, and its commission relied on information from them. The powers governed in their own interest in many ways. So protection was limited, though the idea of international accountability was important.", "Strengths (2); weaknesses (2); judgement (1).")],
    [("The Permanent Mandates Commission", "Reviewed reports from mandatory powers", ["Governed the territories", "Wrote constitutions", "Commanded armies"], "It supervised."),
     ("B mandates included", "Cameroon and Togo", ["Egypt and India", "Namibia only", "Ethiopia"], "Former German colonies in West and Central Africa."),
     ("The League was created in", "1920", ["1945", "1963", "1884"], "After WWI."),
     ("A weakness of the League was", "No power of enforcement", ["Too many soldiers", "Too much money", "Too much authority"], "Its decisions relied on states."),
     ("The mandatory power for most of Cameroon was", "France", ["Germany", "Spain", "Italy"], "France held about four-fifths.")],
    (table(["Body", "Years", "Role"], [["League of Nations", "1920–46", "Mandates"], ["United Nations", "1945–", "Trusteeship"], ["Mandates", "1922–46", "British, French"]], 440, 12, 32),
     "From League mandates to UN trusteeship.", "A table of the League, the UN and the mandates with approximate years and roles."),
    ["Verify: League 1920 (dissolved 1946); mandates confirmed 1922; classification as B mandates; Permanent Mandates Commission.", "French administration from Yaoundé: check the details used in the syllabus."])

L = lesson(ch, "admin", "Colonial administration: indirect and direct rule",
    ["Explain indirect rule and direct rule (assimilation)", "Compare British and French colonial administration", "Assess their effects on Africans"],
    [("definition", "Indirect rule", "**Indirect rule**, associated with Frederick Lugard in Nigeria, governed through existing African rulers, called **Native Authorities**, supervised by British officials. It was cheap, since few officials were needed, and kept some traditional institutions, but it gave power to some chiefs, created chiefs where none existed, and limited modern change."),
     ("retenir", "Direct rule and assimilation", "French rule was more **direct**: a governor and district officers (*commandants*), with chiefs used as agents of the administration. The policy of **assimilation** aimed to make a small educated elite French in language and culture. Africans under the *indigénat* code were subject to special penalties and labour duties."),
     ("retenir", "Comparison", "Both systems aimed to control territory, tax, and organise the export of raw materials. British rule left more space for local rulers and for local languages, while French rule promoted the French language and centralised power. In practice the differences were not absolute: both used chiefs and both used force."),
     ("attention", "Do not overgeneralise", "- British rule was not always ‘indirect’: where there were no strong chiefs the system was harder to apply.\n- French assimilation reached few Africans: most were not citizens.\n- Treat the labels as tools, not as exact descriptions of each place.")],
    [("Example 1 — Advantage and disadvantage", "Give one advantage and one disadvantage of indirect rule.",
      ["Advantage: cheap and kept local institutions.", "Disadvantage: concentrated power in chiefs who might misuse it, and slowed the development of new forms of representation."], "Cost saving versus abuse and limited change."),
     ("Example 2 — Comparison", "Compare the language policies of British and French rule.",
      ["Britain allowed local languages in primary schooling and used English for higher levels.", "France promoted French in schools and government.", "Both created elites using the colonial language."], "British: more local languages; French: assimilation through French.")],
    [M("Indirect rule is linked to", "Frederick Lugard", ["Otto von Bismarck", "Kwame Nkrumah", "Leopold II"], "He applied it in Northern Nigeria."),
     TF("Under assimilation, the French aimed to turn some Africans into French in culture.", True, "A small elite was targeted."),
     MT("Match each term to its meaning.", [("Native Authority", "African ruler working under colonial supervision"), ("Commandant", "French district officer"), ("Indigénat", "Legal code with special penalties for Africans"), ("Assimilation", "Making Africans French in language and culture")], "Key terms."),
     M("A reason why Britain used indirect rule was", "It was cheaper", ["It gave quick independence", "It had no officers", "It was required by the UN"], "Fewer British officials were needed."),
     O("Explain two weaknesses of indirect rule.", "It could strengthen unrepresentative or unpopular chiefs and create new ones, weakening accountability. It was difficult to apply where societies had no centralised chiefs and it limited the training of educated Africans for modern administration.", "Two weaknesses (2); explanation (2)."),
     O("Compare British and French colonial policies and assess their effects on African political development.", "Britain used local rulers and kept local languages in schools; France centralised and promoted French culture. Both extracted resources and limited African political rights. British methods strengthened traditional rulers; French methods created a small educated elite and weakened chiefs. The effects shaped the leaders and political parties that appeared later, but the contrasts were not absolute.", "British approach (1); French approach (1); similarities (1); effects (2).")],
    [("Native Authorities were", "African rulers supervised by the British", ["French commandants", "German governors", "UN officials"], "Used in indirect rule."),
     ("Assimilation was mainly a", "French policy", ["British policy", "German policy", "Portuguese policy only"], "Associated with France."),
     ("A strength of indirect rule was", "Low cost", ["No chiefs", "No taxes", "No laws"], "Few officers needed."),
     ("A weakness was", "It could support unrepresentative chiefs", ["It trained many officers", "It ended trade", "It gave the vote to all"], "Local rulers might abuse power."),
     ("The indigénat was", "A French code of special penalties", ["A British school", "A trade union", "A railway"], "Applied to Africans without citizenship.")],
    (table(["Feature", "British", "French"], [["Approach", "Indirect", "Direct"], ["Chiefs", "Native Authorities", "Agents"], ["Aim", "Cheap order", "Assimilation"]], 440, 13, 32),
     "British and French colonial approaches compared.", "A three-column table comparing approach, chiefs and aim for British and French colonial rule."),
    ["Verify: Lugard and indirect rule in Northern Nigeria; terms Native Authority, commandant, indigénat; how the system worked in the British Cameroons (administered with Nigeria).", "Check the weight the syllabus gives to Cameroon-specific examples."])

un_fig = boxes(["General Assembly", "Security Council", "Secretariat", "International Court of Justice", "Economic and Social Council", "Trusteeship Council"], 440, "blue", 12, 2, 40, 10)
L = lesson(ch, "un-trusteeship", "The United Nations and the Trusteeship system",
    ["Describe the creation, aims and organs of the UN", "Explain the Trusteeship system and its application to Cameroon", "Assess the UN’s role in decolonisation"],
    [("definition", "The UN", "The **United Nations** was founded in **1945**, after the Second World War, to maintain peace and security, promote human rights and encourage cooperation. Its Charter was signed in San Francisco in June 1945 and came into force in October 1945. Main organs: **General Assembly**, **Security Council** (five permanent members with a veto), **Secretariat**, **International Court of Justice**, **Economic and Social Council** and the **Trusteeship Council**."),
     ("retenir", "Trusteeship", "The UN replaced League mandates with **trusteeship** for former mandates and some other territories. Administering powers had to promote the political, economic and social progress of the inhabitants towards **self-government or independence**, and report to the Trusteeship Council, which sent **visiting missions**. In **1946** the British and French Cameroons became trust territories."),
     ("retenir", "Impact on decolonisation", "The UN gave a forum where anti-colonial views were heard, and the General Assembly passed the Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples in 1960. UN supervision, visiting missions and petitions gave Cameroonians a way to raise their demands, and UN plebiscites in 1961 settled the future of the British Cameroons."),
     ("pieges", "Don’t confuse", "- UN Trusteeship (1946) replaced the League’s mandates; the administering powers were the same.\n- Trusteeship aimed at self-government or independence; mandates had a vaguer aim.\n- The Security Council’s five permanent members are not elected: they hold a veto.")],
    [("Example 1 — Change from mandate to trusteeship", "State two differences between mandates and trusteeship.",
      ["Trusteeship had the explicit aim of self-government or independence.", "It was supervised by the Trusteeship Council with visiting missions and petitions.", "Mandates were supervised by the Permanent Mandates Commission with less direct access."], "Clear aim of self-rule and stronger supervision."),
     ("Example 2 — Role of visiting missions", "Explain how UN visiting missions helped Cameroonians.",
      ["Missions visited the territories and reported on conditions.", "Petitions allowed individuals and groups to raise concerns.", "UN debates kept the question of the territories’ future on the international agenda."], "A channel for opinions and international attention.")],
    [M("The UN was founded in", "1945", ["1920", "1960", "1963"], "The Charter entered into force in October 1945."),
     TF("The British and French Cameroons became trust territories in 1946.", True, "Trusteeship agreements were approved in 1946."),
     MT("Match each UN organ to its role.", [("General Assembly", "All members meet and vote"), ("Security Council", "Peace and security; permanent members have a veto"), ("International Court of Justice", "Settles legal disputes between states"), ("Trusteeship Council", "Supervised trust territories")], "Standard roles."),
     N("The UN started in 1945. The Trusteeship agreements for Cameroon were in 1946. How many years after the UN started?", 1, "1946 − 1945 = 1.", 0, "years"),
     O("State three aims of the United Nations.", "To maintain international peace and security; to promote friendly relations among nations and respect for human rights; to encourage cooperation to solve economic, social and humanitarian problems.", "One mark each, max 3."),
     O("Assess the contribution of the UN Trusteeship system to the progress of Cameroon towards self-government.", "Trusteeship set the goal of self-government or independence and allowed supervision through visiting missions and petitions, so that Cameroonians could raise demands. UN debates and plebiscites decided the later political status of the territories. However, administering powers had considerable control and the UN had limited power to force changes. The UN’s role was therefore important but limited.", "Contribution (2); limitations (2); judgement (1).")],
    [("The Security Council has", "Five permanent members with a veto", ["Ten permanent members", "No veto", "Only African members"], "Standard structure."),
     ("The Trusteeship Council", "Supervised trust territories", ["Ran the ICJ", "Managed the Secretariat", "Set oil prices"], "Visited and reviewed territories."),
     ("The UN Charter was signed in", "San Francisco", ["Berlin", "Versailles", "Addis Ababa"], "June 1945."),
     ("Trusteeship aimed at", "Self-government or independence", ["Permanent rule", "Free trade only", "Military alliance"], "A goal stated in the Charter."),
     ("The International Court of Justice", "Settles legal disputes between states", ["Controls trade", "Writes books", "Governs colonies"], "Based in The Hague.")],
    (un_fig, "Main organs of the United Nations.", "Six boxes naming the six main UN organs."),
    ["Verify: UN founded 1945 (Charter in force 24 October 1945); five permanent members; trusteeship agreements for Cameroons approved 1946; Declaration of 1960; 1961 plebiscites.", "UN processes in the Cameroons are politically sensitive: follow the syllabus and avoid opinions on how they should be judged."])

p.write()
